  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Black Tooth (Phellodon niger) is a small, tooth fungus belonging to the Bankeraceae family, characterized by its dark coloration and delicate spine-like structures beneath the cap. Found primarily in coniferous and mixed forests of New England, this species is inedible and primarily of scientific and mycological interest.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Dark coloration",
      "description": "Cap and spines are distinctly black or very dark brown, making it one of the darkest tooth fungi",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Spine arrangement",
      "description": "Spines run down the stem and are decurrent; they easily separate from the cap flesh when mature",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "Typically 2-5 cm in diameter with a delicate, fragile structure",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Black Tooth grows on mossy soil, wood debris, and rich humus in cool, moist coniferous forests, particularly under spruce and fir trees throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its sinister appearance, Phellodon niger is harmless to humans and has historically been studied by mycologists interested in the remarkable diversity of tooth fungi within the Bankeraceae family.",
